this program is about the only way of  collecting meteorites in a very  scientific and methodical way  you spend so much time preparing once  you get down there you don't know  whether you can do this or not and then  the first time you find the meteorite  you say yes i can do this  my name is konstantin seng i'm at  southwest research institute in boulder  colorado i'm a planetary scientist  studying planetary atmospheres  we spent a total of two months on the  ice and we collected approximately 569  meteorites  the scientific expedition is called  anzmat short for antarctic search for  meteorites  it's nasa funded and what we're trying  to understand is how formation of solar  system began and how the planets evolved  the way they looked today three  tranquility the beauty the awesomeness  there's no hyperbole that's too great  for antarctica  the ice is crystal blue the snow is  thick  and the sun is brilliant  when we're on the ice field we live very  basic we live in tents with power  supplied by solar panels and chipping  ice water  the reason why we go to antarctica is  because these are black rocks on a white  surface so they're much easier to find  as they go through the atmosphere they  start to burn up and they create this  black fusion crust and that's very  distinct from any terrestrial rock  by studying meteorites we're actually  looking at the primordial history of the  solar system this is a direct piece of  evidence how the planets evolved and  form and how did life evolve on the  earth  no one gets to go out there except for  scientists and very rich folk so  it was a huge privilege to go out and  experience something that is  life-changing  you
 

